@@s001 Brute force and branching
@@s002 ora:bit of k in this step,ok:valid subset,dato:subsets already seen
@@s003 Brute force: I try all the subsets of 3 elements. The number k from 0 to 2³ − 1 = 7 is a <b>mask</b>: its bit i says whether element i is in the subset.
@@s004 k = 0: bits 000, empty subset. k = 1: bits 001 (bit 0 on), subset {1}. Bit 0 corresponds to element 1.
@@s005 k = 3: bits 011, subset {1, 2}. With k & (1 &lt;&lt; i) I check bit i in constant time.
@@s006 The same for all: 8 subsets. For each one I check the condition in polynomial time and keep the best. Cost <code>O(2ⁿ · poly(n))</code>: exponential in n, but always correct.
@@s007 Brute force and branching
@@s008 When a problem is NP-hard, the only guaranteed way to the exact solution is to try the candidates. Brute force enumerates them all, branching branches only where needed. The cost is exponential, but it often depends on the size of the solution, not on n.
@@s009 Enumerating with bits
@@s010 A subset of <code>n</code> elements is represented by a number <code>k</code> from 0 to <code>2ⁿ − 1</code>: bit <code>i</code> of <code>k</code> says whether element <code>i</code> is inside. In Java: <code>k &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; i)</code> is non-zero if bit i is on. For each subset you check the condition and keep the best.
@@s011 Bit masks: k = 0 … 7
@@s012 Branching: in or out
@@s013 Branching does the same thing as recursion: for an element, two branches, in or out. With pruning, the branches that cannot improve are discarded.
@@s014 Example: vertex cover with small k. Take an edge <code>u–v</code>: at least one of the two is in the cover. Branch on <code>G − u</code> with <code>k − 1</code> and on <code>G − v</code> with <code>k − 1</code>. Depth <code>k</code>, <code>2ᵏ</code> branches: exponential in <b>k</b>, not in n.
@@s015 When it is useful and why it is correct
@@s016 Every problem in NP has a <b>certificate</b> verifiable in polynomial time. So trying all the certificates is always correct, only slow. To understand the costs: <code>O(2ⁿ · poly(n))</code> for the subsets, <code>O(n! · poly(n))</code> for the permutations, <code>O(2ᵏ · kn)</code> for the branching on vertex cover.
@@s017 The code, side by side
@@s018 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
@@s019 <a href="../codice/forza-bruta.html"><b>→ Java code for this topic</b></a>
@@s020 Permutations in lexicographic order
@@s021 A <b>permutation</b> of 1, 2, 3 is a way of writing the three numbers in a row: 123, 132, 213, 231, 312, 321. There are 3! = 6. The <b>lexicographic order</b> is the dictionary one: you compare the first number, then the second, and so on.
@@s022 To go from the permutation <code>p</code> to the next one, there are three moves:
@@s023 From the right I look for the first point where the row <b>stops increasing</b>: the position <code>i</code> with <code>p[i] &lt; p[i+1]</code>. The suffix to the right of <code>i</code> is decreasing: you cannot increase it by staying inside.
@@s024 The number at <code>i</code> (the <b>pivot</b>) must be increased: I look from the right for the smallest number greater than <code>p[i]</code>, and I swap them.
@@s025 The suffix to the right of <code>i</code> is decreasing: I reverse it, so it becomes as small as possible (increasing).
@@s026 From 132 to the next one, 213, in three moves
@@s027 Generating all n! permutations costs O(n!) in total: on average each step touches a very short suffix. With n = 13 there are more than 6 billion: brute force only works for small n.
@@s028 At the exam
@@s029 At the exam
@@s030 NP-hard problem with a small instance, or with a small parameter (k) → brute force or branching. Phrase: <i>"Enumerate all subsets using bit masks; each subset is checked in polynomial time. Time O(2ⁿ · poly(n))."</i>
